-- title:
--   Standard étale presentation at étale coordinates over ℂ
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain and a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra of finite type, and assume $S$ is smooth over $\mathbb{C}$ in the sense of `Algebra.Smooth` (formally smooth and of finite presentation). Let $n$ be a natural number and suppose the $S$-module rank of the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}} =$ `KaehlerDifferential ℂ S` equals $n$ (as cardinals). Let $\sigma_0 : S \to \mathbb{C}$ be a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphism and let $t : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to S$ be an $n$-tuple of elements, and assume that the submodule $\mathfrak{m}\,\Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}} + \sum_i S\,\mathrm{d}t_i$ is all of $\Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}}$, where $\mathfrak{m}$ is the kernel of $\sigma_0$ and $\mathrm{d}$ is the universal derivation; that is, the submodule generated by the image of $\mathfrak{m} \otimes \Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}}$ together with the span of the $\mathrm{d}t_i$ is the whole module. The conclusion is that there exists $g \in S$ with $\sigma_0(g) \neq 0$ such that the localisation $S_g =$ `Localization.Away g`, regarded as an algebra over the polynomial ring $\mathbb{C}[X_i : i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n]$ via the evaluation $X_i \mapsto t_i$ followed by the localisation map $S \to S_g$, satisfies `Algebra.IsStandardEtale`, i.e. admits a standard étale presentation over $\mathbb{C}[X_1,\dots,X_n]$.
--
--   This is the local structure theorem for étale morphisms in the shape used for smooth complex affine varieties: a choice of $n$ functions whose differentials generate $\Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}}$ at a $\mathbb{C}$-point makes $S$, after localising away from a single function not vanishing at that point, standard étale over the polynomial ring in $n$ variables. It is cited by [`Algebra.exists_bijOn_eval_differentiableOn_pi_of_smooth_of_kaehlerDifferential`](thm.html#Algebra.exists_bijOn_eval_differentiableOn_pi_of_smooth_of_kaehlerDifferential), where the standard étale presentation is turned into local complex-analytic coordinates on the $\mathbb{C}$-points of $S$ near $\sigma_0$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Algebra_exists_isStandardEtale_mvPolynomial_localizationAway_of_smooth_of_kaehlerDifferential.lean

